  "body": "One difference between HTML5 specs is the SVG spec they defer to. WHATWG uses [SVG Tiny 1.2](http://www.w3.org/TR/SVGTiny12/), and W3C uses [SVG 1.1](http://www.w3.org/TR/SVG/).\n\nAs far as what browsers actually do, their implementations are closer to SVG 1.1, with the odd Tiny 1.2 feature thrown in:\n- Chrome uses the `bufferedRendering` attribute as an optimization hint\n- IE9+ use the `focusable` attribute to disallow focusing\n- Browsers allow the `class` attribute on anything, unlike 1.1, where it's not a global attribute\n\nAnd doubtless other little weird warts and hairs. However, much of SVG Tiny 1.2 is unimplemented, and has no interest to be. The microDOM, for example. Many of these features are being dropped for SVG2, and have no future.\n\nOne specific problem is how Tiny 1.2 treats the content model of the `<title>` and `<desc>` elements; [they're specified to only be plain text](http://www.w3.org/TR/SVGTiny12/struct.html#TitleAndDescriptionElements). However, the current HTML5 spec delineates `<title>` and `<desc>` as \"[HTML integration points](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#html-integration-point),\" and [specifically restricts `<title>` only to phrasing content](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#svg-0).\n\nKeeping `<desc>` plaintext would prevent authors including links to fragments on other parts of the document, which would be very helpful for assistive technology users.\n",
